WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.460 --> 00:04.940
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

00:10.200 --> 00:13.650
Good afternoon.

00:13.650 --> 00:21.230
The Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) concluded a two-day meeting

00:21.230 --> 00:22.870
earlier today.

00:22.870 --> 00:24.580
As you already know from our statement,

00:24.580 --> 00:28.460
the Committee decided today to keep the target range for the federal funds rate

00:28.460 --> 00:33.810
at 0 to ¼ percent and to make no change in either its asset purchase program

00:33.810 --> 00:37.290
or its forward guidance regarding the federal funds rate target.

00:37.290 --> 00:41.600
I will discuss the rationales for our decision in a moment.

00:41.600 --> 00:45.010
Economic growth has generally been proceeding at a moderate pace,

00:45.010 --> 00:50.660
with continued- albeit somewhat uneven-improvement in labor market conditions.

00:50.660 --> 00:53.780
Of course, to say that the job market has improved does not imply

00:53.780 --> 00:56.950
that current conditions are satisfactory.

00:56.950 --> 01:00.410
Notably, at 7.3 percent, the unemployment rate remains well

01:00.410 --> 01:02.930
above acceptable levels.

01:02.930 --> 01:05.690
Long-term unemployment and underemployment remain high.

01:05.690 --> 01:09.890
And we have seen ongoing declines in labor force participation,

01:09.890 --> 01:13.500
which likely reflects discouragement on the part of many potential workers

01:13.500 --> 01:17.510
as well as longer-term influences, such as the aging of the population.

01:17.510 --> 01:21.440
In the Committee's assessment, the downside risks to growth have diminished,

01:21.440 --> 01:25.280
on net, over the past year, reflecting, among other factors,

01:25.280 --> 01:28.350
somewhat better economic and financial conditions in Europe

01:28.350 --> 01:30.460
and increased confidence on the part of households

01:30.460 --> 01:34.780
and firms in the staying power of the U.S. recovery.

01:34.780 --> 01:38.990
However, the tightening of financial conditions observed in recent months,

01:38.990 --> 01:42.000
if sustained, could slow the pace of improvement

01:42.000 --> 01:44.310
in the economy and the labor market.

01:44.310 --> 01:48.310
In addition, federal fiscal policy continues to be an important restraint

01:48.310 --> 01:52.920
on growth and a source of downside risk.

01:52.920 --> 01:56.990
Apart from some fluctuations due primarily to changes in oil prices,

01:56.990 --> 02:00.340
inflation has continued to run below the Committee's 2 percent

02:00.340 --> 02:02.700
longer-term objective.

02:02.700 --> 02:06.850
The Committee recognizes that inflation persistently below its objective could

02:06.850 --> 02:09.220
pose risks to economic performance,

02:09.220 --> 02:12.570
and we will continue to monitor inflation developments closely.

02:12.570 --> 02:16.140
However, the unwinding of some transitory factors has led

02:16.140 --> 02:19.450
to moderately higher inflation recently, as expected.

02:19.450 --> 02:22.690
And, with longer-term inflation expectations well anchored,

02:22.690 --> 02:25.980
the Committee anticipates that inflation will gradually move back toward

02:25.980 --> 02:27.090
2 percent.

02:27.090 --> 02:29.840
In conjunction with this meeting,

02:29.840 --> 02:33.960
the 17 participants in our policy discussions-5 Board members

02:33.960 --> 02:38.300
and 12 Reserve Bank presidents-submitted individual economic projections.

02:38.300 --> 02:43.490
As always, each participant's projections are conditioned on his or her own view

02:43.490 --> 02:46.250
of appropriate monetary policy.

02:46.250 --> 02:52.520
Also, at this meeting, we extended the horizon of our projections through 2016.

02:52.520 --> 02:55.480
Generally, the projections of individual participants show that they continue

02:55.480 --> 02:59.540
to expect moderate economic growth, picking up over time,

02:59.540 --> 03:03.340
as well as gradual progress towards levels of unemployment and inflation,

03:03.340 --> 03:06.030
consistent with the Federal Reserve's statutory mandate

03:06.030 --> 03:09.990
to foster maximum employment and price stability.

03:09.990 --> 03:11.850
More specifically, participants' projections

03:11.850 --> 03:18.560
for economic growth have a central tendency of 2.0 to 2.3 percent for 2013,

03:18.560 --> 03:27.680
rising to 2.9 to 3.1 percent in 2014 and 2.5 to 3.3 percent in 2016.

03:27.680 --> 03:30.150
For the unemployment rate, the central tendency of projections

03:30.150 --> 03:35.410
for the fourth quarter of each year is 7.1 to 7.3 percent for 2013,

03:35.410 --> 03:41.080
declining to 6.4 to 6.8 percent in 2014 and, by 2016,

03:41.080 --> 03:45.560
to 5.4 to 5.9 percent-about the longer-run normal level

03:45.560 --> 03:46.930
for the unemployment rate.

03:46.930 --> 03:50.570
Most participants see inflation gradually increasing

03:50.570 --> 03:53.430
from its current low level toward the Committee's longer-run objective

03:53.430 --> 03:55.160
of 2 percent.

03:55.160 --> 03:59.620
The central tendency of their projections for inflation is 1.1 to 1.2 percent

03:59.620 --> 04:09.150
for this year, 1.3 to 1.8 percent for 2014, and 1.7 to 2.0 percent in 2016.

04:09.150 --> 04:12.740
With unemployment still elevated and inflation projected

04:12.740 --> 04:14.670
to run below the Committee's longer-run objective,

04:14.670 --> 04:19.290
the Committee is continuing its highly accommodative policies.

04:19.290 --> 04:22.280
As you know, in normal times, the Committee eases monetary policy

04:22.280 --> 04:23.610
by lowering its target for the short-

04:23.610 --> 04:27.250
term policy interest rate, the federal funds rate.

04:27.250 --> 04:29.470
However, the target range for the federal funds rate,

04:29.470 --> 04:34.880
currently at 0 to ¼ percent, cannot be lowered meaningfully further.

04:34.880 --> 04:38.460
Accordingly, the Committee has been providing policy support to the economy

04:38.460 --> 04:42.610
through two complementary methods: by purchasing and holding Treasury securities

04:42.610 --> 04:46.820
and agency mortgage-backed securities and by communicating the Committee's plans

04:46.820 --> 04:50.600
for setting the federal funds rate target over the medium term.

04:50.600 --> 04:54.650
I'll discuss these tools in turn, beginning with our program of asset purchases.

04:54.650 --> 04:59.650
In September 2012, the FOMC initiated a program

04:59.650 --> 05:04.230
of purchasing $40 billion per month in agency mortgage-backed securities,

05:04.230 --> 05:08.350
in addition to the $45 billion per month in longer-term Treasury securities

05:08.350 --> 05:12.910
that we were already acquiring as part of our maturity extension program.

05:12.910 --> 05:16.110
We stated that, subject to our ongoing assessment of the efficacy

05:16.110 --> 05:19.230
and costs of the program, purchases would continue

05:19.230 --> 05:23.340
until we saw a substantial improvement in the outlook for the labor market

05:23.340 --> 05:25.000
in a context of price stability.

05:25.000 --> 05:28.700
In December 2012, we announced that we would continue

05:28.700 --> 05:32.590
to purchase $45 billion per month in longer-term Treasuries

05:32.590 --> 05:36.750
after the maturity extension program ended later that month.

05:36.750 --> 05:41.090
Thus, our total purchases of longer-term securities were maintained

05:41.090 --> 05:44.710
at $85 billion per month, in addition to the reinvestment

05:44.710 --> 05:48.270
or rolling over of maturing securities on our balance sheet.

05:48.270 --> 05:53.500
The Committee agreed today to continue asset purchases at that rate,

05:53.500 --> 05:56.790
subject to the same conditions that we laid out a year ago.

05:56.790 --> 06:01.740
Because the Committee tied its asset purchases to the outlook

06:01.740 --> 06:07.000
for the labor market, it's important to assess how that outlook has evolved.

06:07.000 --> 06:10.290
As I noted earlier, conditions in the job market today are still far

06:10.290 --> 06:13.070
from what all of us would like to see.

06:13.070 --> 06:16.060
Nevertheless, meaningful progress has been made in the year

06:16.060 --> 06:19.070
since we announced the asset purchase program.

06:19.070 --> 06:23.000
For example, the unemployment rate has fallen from 8.1 percent at the time

06:23.000 --> 06:26.560
of our announcement to 7.3 percent today,

06:26.560 --> 06:30.200
and about 2.3 million private-sector jobs have been created

06:30.200 --> 06:32.920
over the same period.

06:32.920 --> 06:37.520
Over the past 12 months, aggregate hours of work are up by about 2.4 percent,

06:37.520 --> 06:41.590
weekly new claims for unemployment insurance have fallen by about 50,000,

06:41.590 --> 06:46.660
and surveys suggest that households perceive jobs as more readily available.

06:46.660 --> 06:50.550
Importantly, these gains were achieved despite substantial fiscal headwinds,

06:50.550 --> 06:53.580
which are likely slowing economic growth this year by a percentage point,

06:53.580 --> 06:58.080
or more, and reducing employment by hundreds of thousands of jobs.

06:58.080 --> 07:02.260
Not all labor market developments over the past year were positive, however;

07:02.260 --> 07:07.060
notably, the labor force participation rate fell by about 0.3 percentage points

07:07.060 --> 07:10.830
and real wages remained about flat.

07:10.830 --> 07:15.140
In light of this cumulative progress, the FOMC concluded at our June meeting

07:15.140 --> 07:17.830
that the criterion of substantial improvement in the outlook

07:17.830 --> 07:22.830
for the labor market might well be met over the subsequent year or so.

07:22.830 --> 07:25.570
Accordingly, the Committee sought to provide more guidance on how the pace

07:25.570 --> 07:27.950
of purchases might be adjusted over time.

07:27.950 --> 07:31.890
The Committee anticipated in June that, subject to certain conditions,

07:31.890 --> 07:34.890
it might be appropriate to begin to moderate the pace

07:34.890 --> 07:38.880
of purchases later this year, continuing to reduce the pace of purchases

07:38.880 --> 07:42.130
in measured steps through the first half of next year,

07:42.130 --> 07:46.050
and ending purchases around midyear 2014.

07:46.050 --> 07:50.080
However, we also made clear at that time that adjustments to the pace

07:50.080 --> 07:52.860
of purchases would depend importantly on the evolution

07:52.860 --> 07:55.130
of the economic outlook-in particular,

07:55.130 --> 07:59.030
on the receipt of evidence supporting the Committee's expectation that gains

07:59.030 --> 08:00.950
in the labor market will be sustained

08:00.950 --> 08:04.940
and that inflation is moving back towards its 2 percent objective over time.

08:04.940 --> 08:08.490
At the meeting concluded earlier today,

08:08.490 --> 08:10.830
the sense of the Committee was that the broad contours

08:10.830 --> 08:14.640
of the medium-term economic outlook-including economic growth sufficient

08:14.640 --> 08:17.180
to support ongoing gains in the labor market,

08:17.180 --> 08:20.140
and inflation moving towards its objective-were close

08:20.140 --> 08:22.950
to the views it held in June.

08:22.950 --> 08:25.700
But in evaluating whether a modest reduction in the pace

08:25.700 --> 08:29.150
of asset purchases would be appropriate at this meeting, however,

08:29.150 --> 08:31.410
the Committee concluded that the economic data do not

08:31.410 --> 08:35.140
yet provide sufficient confirmation of its baseline outlook

08:35.140 --> 08:37.750
to warrant such a reduction.

08:37.750 --> 08:40.280
Moreover, the Committee has some concern that the rapid tightening

08:40.280 --> 08:43.300
of financial conditions in recent months could have the effect

08:43.300 --> 08:47.320
of slowing growth, as I noted earlier, a concern that would be exacerbated

08:47.320 --> 08:50.170
if conditions tightened further.

08:50.170 --> 08:52.050
Finally, the extent of the effects

08:52.050 --> 08:54.910
of restrictive fiscal policies remain unclear,

08:54.910 --> 08:59.030
and upcoming fiscal debates may involve additional risks to financial markets

08:59.030 --> 09:01.560
and to the broader economy.

09:01.560 --> 09:05.350
In light of these uncertainties, the Committee decided to await more evidence

09:05.350 --> 09:08.460
that the recovery's progress will be sustained before adjusting the pace

09:08.460 --> 09:09.890
of asset purchases.

09:09.890 --> 09:13.800
The Committee will, of course, continue to monitor economic

09:13.800 --> 09:16.070
and financial developments closely.

09:16.070 --> 09:19.640
As noted in today's statement, in judging when to moderate the pace

09:19.640 --> 09:22.840
of asset purchases, the Committee will, at its coming meetings,

09:22.840 --> 09:25.610
assess whether incoming information continues

09:25.610 --> 09:29.000
to support the Committee's expectation of ongoing improvement

09:29.000 --> 09:32.160
in labor market conditions and inflation moving back towards its

09:32.160 --> 09:34.150
longer-run objective.

09:34.150 --> 09:38.840
However, as we have said, and as today's decision underscores,

09:38.840 --> 09:42.510
asset purchases are not on a preset course.

09:42.510 --> 09:45.220
The Committee's decisions about their pace will remain contingent

09:45.220 --> 09:48.940
on the economic outlook and on the Committee's ongoing assessment

09:48.940 --> 09:52.260
of the likely efficacy and costs of the program.

09:52.260 --> 09:58.030
Let me turn now to the FOMC's forward guidance regarding the federal funds rate.

09:58.030 --> 10:00.310
The Committee again reaffirmed its expectation

10:00.310 --> 10:04.080
that the current exceptionally low range for the funds rate will be appropriate

10:04.080 --> 10:08.030
at least as long as the unemployment rate remains above 6½ percent,

10:08.030 --> 10:12.060
so long as inflation and inflation expectations remain well behaved

10:12.060 --> 10:14.030
as described in our statement.

10:14.030 --> 10:17.220
As I have noted frequently, the economic conditions we have set

10:17.220 --> 10:23.460
out as preceding any future rate increase are thresholds, not triggers.

10:23.460 --> 10:25.360
For example, a decline in the unemployment rate

10:25.360 --> 10:28.420
to 6½ percent would not lead automatically to an increase

10:28.420 --> 10:31.330
in the federal funds rate target, but would, instead,

10:31.330 --> 10:34.270
indicate only that it had become appropriate for the Committee

10:34.270 --> 10:38.820
to consider whether the broader economic outlook justified such an increase.

10:38.820 --> 10:43.110
The Committee would be unlikely to increase rates if inflation were projected

10:43.110 --> 10:47.140
to remain below our 2 percent objective for some time, for example; and,

10:47.140 --> 10:49.080
in making its assessment, the Committee would also take

10:49.080 --> 10:54.630
into account additional measures of labor market conditions, such as job gains.

10:54.630 --> 10:57.940
Thus, the first increases in short-term rates might not occur

10:57.940 --> 11:02.310
until the unemployment rate is considerably below 6½ percent.

11:02.310 --> 11:06.390
The projections of the path of the federal funds rate

11:06.390 --> 11:09.540
by individual Committee participants are generally consistent

11:09.540 --> 11:11.570
with this guidance.

11:11.570 --> 11:14.020
Although the central tendency of the projected unemployment rate

11:14.020 --> 11:18.150
for the fourth quarter of next year encompasses 6½ percent,

11:18.150 --> 11:24.320
12 of the 17 participants expect the first rate increase to take place in 2015

11:24.320 --> 11:29.080
and two expect it to occur in 2016.

11:29.080 --> 11:32.690
Most participants also see the funds rate target rising only very slowly

11:32.690 --> 11:36.760
after the process of removing policy accommodation begins.

11:36.760 --> 11:41.710
The median projected funds rate for the end of 2015 is 1 percent.

11:41.710 --> 11:44.290
And notably, although the central tendencies of the projections

11:44.290 --> 11:48.570
for both inflation and the unemployment rate in 2016 are close

11:48.570 --> 11:51.620
to the longer-run normal values for those variables,

11:51.620 --> 11:53.910
the median projection for the federal funds rate at the end

11:53.910 --> 11:58.620
of 2016 is 2 percent, well below the longer-run normal value

11:58.620 --> 12:04.430
for the federal funds rate of 4 percent or so projected by most participants.

12:04.430 --> 12:07.380
Committee participants generally believe that,

12:07.380 --> 12:10.980
because the headwinds to recovery will abate only gradually,

12:10.980 --> 12:13.420
achieving and maintaining maximum employment

12:13.420 --> 12:17.450
and price stability will require a patient policy approach

12:17.450 --> 12:18.720
that involves keeping the target

12:18.720 --> 12:23.820
for the federal funds rate below its longer-run normal value for some time.

12:24.960 --> 12:28.560
Let me close by noting that although the FOMC is employing two instruments

12:28.560 --> 12:31.660
of policy-asset purchases and forward guidance

12:31.660 --> 12:34.570
about short-term interest rates-the overall stance

12:34.570 --> 12:39.830
of monetary policy is what matters for growth, jobs, and inflation.

12:39.830 --> 12:42.340
Our program of asset purchases was set up a year ago

12:42.340 --> 12:45.730
to help achieve a substantial improvement in the outlook for the labor market

12:45.730 --> 12:47.870
in the context of price stability,

12:47.870 --> 12:51.070
relative to conditions when the program was initiated,

12:51.070 --> 12:54.400
and we have made progress toward meeting that criterion.

12:54.400 --> 12:58.890
However, even after asset purchases are wound down-which we will do in a manner

12:58.890 --> 13:01.020
that is both deliberate and dependent

13:01.020 --> 13:04.820
on the incoming economic data-the Federal Reserve's rates guidance

13:04.820 --> 13:07.820
and its ongoing holdings of securities will ensure

13:07.820 --> 13:10.760
that monetary policy remains highly accommodative,

13:10.760 --> 13:14.310
consistent with an aggressive pursuit of our mandated objectives

13:14.310 --> 13:17.640
of maximum employment and price stability.

13:17.640 --> 13:20.250
Thank you, and I'd be glad to take your questions.

13:20.250 --> 13:23.670
PEDRO DA COSTA.

13:23.670 --> 13:24.390
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

13:24.390 --> 13:26.010
Pedro da Costa from Reuters.

13:26.010 --> 13:30.790
You cite meaningful progress on the labor market both on the unemployment front

13:30.790 --> 13:33.500
and in terms of payroll growth.

13:33.500 --> 13:37.340
But much of the decline in the unemployment rate has been due, as you know,

13:37.340 --> 13:39.020
to the decline in participation.

13:39.020 --> 13:42.500
So my question to you is-and also on the payroll front,

13:42.500 --> 13:44.600
some people would argue that, while there has been growth,

13:44.600 --> 13:47.630
it hasn't been strong enough to keep up with population growth

13:47.630 --> 13:49.610
and make up the gap that we have from the recession.

13:49.610 --> 13:53.200
So, how high do you think the jobless rate would be if it were not

13:53.200 --> 13:54.640
for the decline in participation?

13:54.640 --> 13:57.520
I've heard estimates as high as 10 to 11 percent.

13:57.520 --> 14:00.780
And could you put the labor market in that context?

14:00.780 --> 14:01.590
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

14:01.590 --> 14:01.830
Certainly.

14:01.830 --> 14:06.980
So, I think there is a cyclical component to participation, and,

14:06.980 --> 14:14.050
in that respect, the unemployment rate understates the amount of sort

14:14.050 --> 14:17.230
of true unemployment, if you will, in the economy.

14:17.230 --> 14:23.450
But on the other hand, there is also a downward trend in participation

14:23.450 --> 14:27.270
in our economy, which is arising from factors that have been going

14:27.270 --> 14:30.450
on for some time, including an aging population,

14:30.450 --> 14:33.660
lower participation by prime-age males,

14:33.660 --> 14:36.160
fewer women in the labor force-other factors

14:36.160 --> 14:39.340
which aren't really related to this recession.

14:39.340 --> 14:42.700
Over the last year, the unemployment rate has dropped

14:42.700 --> 14:45.010
by eight-tenths of a percentage point.

14:45.010 --> 14:47.820
The participation rate has dropped by three-tenths of a percentage point,

14:47.820 --> 14:50.500
which is pretty close to the trend.

14:50.500 --> 14:55.150
So, in other words, I think it would be fair to say that most of the improvement

14:55.150 --> 14:58.790
in the unemployment rate-not all, but most of it in the last year-is due

14:58.790 --> 15:02.440
to job creation rather than lower participation.

15:02.440 --> 15:07.800
I would also note that if you look at the broader measures of unemployment

15:07.800 --> 15:13.270
that the BLS publishes, including part-time work, including discouraged workers

15:13.270 --> 15:16.620
and so on, you'll see that those rates have fallen about the same amount

15:16.620 --> 15:19.710
as the overall standard civilian unemployment rate.

15:19.710 --> 15:23.480
So I think that there has been progress, and it's obscured to some extent

15:23.480 --> 15:25.550
by the downward trend in participation.

15:25.550 --> 15:30.390
But I also would agree with you that the unemployment rate,

15:30.390 --> 15:34.830
while perhaps the best single indicator of the state of labor market,

15:34.830 --> 15:38.840
is not by itself a fully representative indicator.

15:40.970 --> 15:43.090
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

15:45.190 --> 15:47.210
Binya Appelbaum, the New York Times.

15:47.210 --> 15:50.760
To what extent do you regard yourself as responsible for the tightening

15:50.760 --> 15:52.930
in financial conditions that you noted?

15:52.930 --> 15:56.390
Was it a mistake to talk about tapering in the way that you did in June

15:56.390 --> 15:59.060
and do you stand by your guidance that it will be appropriate?

15:59.060 --> 16:01.700
Do you still expect that it will be appropriate to dial

16:01.700 --> 16:03.850
down asset purchases by the end of this year?

16:03.850 --> 16:06.310
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

16:06.310 --> 16:09.600
So to answer the first part of your question,

16:09.600 --> 16:13.530
I think there's no alternative in making monetary policy but to communicate

16:13.530 --> 16:16.110
as clearly as possible, and that's what we tried to do.

16:16.110 --> 16:23.620
As of June, we had made meaningful progress in labor market conditions,

16:23.620 --> 16:26.830
and the Committee thought that was the time to begin talking

16:26.830 --> 16:32.260
about how the eventual wind-down of the program would take place

16:32.260 --> 16:36.000
and how it would be tied to the evolution of economic variables.

16:36.000 --> 16:42.320
And, in particular, I talked about a proposed strategy that would take

16:42.320 --> 16:46.640
about a year for the total wind-down to take place and which, in turn,

16:46.640 --> 16:53.710
was also fully contingent on the ratification-so to speak-of our outlook,

16:53.710 --> 16:57.030
which included continued improvements in the labor market.

16:57.030 --> 17:01.530
So all of that was very consistent with what we said when we began the program,

17:01.530 --> 17:06.480
that our goal was to achieve a substantial improvement in the outlook

17:06.480 --> 17:09.940
for the labor market, and we needed to communicate how

17:09.940 --> 17:12.500
that was going to be put into practice.

17:12.500 --> 17:19.440
Failing to communicate that information would have risked creating a large

17:19.440 --> 17:22.890
divergence between market expectations, public expectations,

17:22.890 --> 17:25.390
and what the Committee intentions were,

17:25.390 --> 17:29.980
and that could have led to much more serious problems down the road.

17:29.980 --> 17:33.790
So I think the communication was very important.

17:33.790 --> 17:38.350
The general framework-to answer the other part of your question,

17:38.350 --> 17:41.830
the general framework in which we're operating is still the same.

17:41.830 --> 17:45.490
We have a three-part baseline projection,

17:45.490 --> 17:52.300
which involves increasing growth that's picking up over time

17:52.300 --> 17:57.290
as fiscal drag is reduced, continuing gains in the labor market,

17:57.290 --> 18:00.040
and inflation moving back towards objective.

18:00.040 --> 18:02.570
We are looking to see-in the coming meetings,

18:02.570 --> 18:07.440
we'll be looking to see if the data confirm that basic outlook.

18:07.440 --> 18:10.010
If it does, we'll take a first step

18:10.010 --> 18:15.180
at some point-possibly later this year-and then continue so long

18:15.180 --> 18:17.870
as the data are consistent with that continued progress.

18:17.870 --> 18:20.510
And so that basic structure is still in place.

18:20.510 --> 18:22.840
But what I want to emphasize is really two things.

18:22.840 --> 18:28.050
First, as I've said, asset purchases are not on a preset course,

18:28.050 --> 18:29.690
they are conditional on the data.

18:29.690 --> 18:31.810
They've always been conditional on the data.

18:31.810 --> 18:37.990
And, secondly, that even as we move from asset purchases to rate policy

18:37.990 --> 18:41.270
as the principal tool of monetary policy,

18:41.270 --> 18:44.050
it's our intent to maintain a highly accommodative policy

18:44.050 --> 18:47.720
and to provide the support necessary for our economy to recover

18:47.720 --> 18:50.500
and to provide jobs for our citizens.

18:50.500 --> 18:53.400
JON HILSENRATH.

18:55.690 --> 18:58.290
Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

18:58.290 --> 19:01.870
Just to follow up on Binya's question, Mr. Chairman,

19:01.870 --> 19:07.960
you said that you could pull back the purchases possibly later this year.

19:07.960 --> 19:12.410
You sound a little bit less certain that it's going to happen later this year.

19:12.410 --> 19:15.840
So I'd like you-to ask you to talk a little bit more about your conviction

19:15.840 --> 19:20.040
about whether these are like-the pullback is likely to start this year,

19:20.040 --> 19:21.090
where do you stand on that?

19:21.090 --> 19:26.140
And I also don't think I heard you mentioned that 7 percent unemployment number

19:26.140 --> 19:29.640
that you've talked-you talked about back in June.

19:29.640 --> 19:33.090
That was the rate that was-the unemployment rate that was supposed to prevail

19:33.090 --> 19:36.050
when the Fed was done doing this.

19:36.050 --> 19:38.320
Is that no longer operative?

19:38.320 --> 19:40.100
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

19:40.100 --> 19:46.310
So there is no fixed calendar-schedule, I really have to emphasize that.

19:46.310 --> 19:50.520
If the data confirm our basic outlook,

19:50.520 --> 19:53.270
if we gain more confidence in that outlook and we believe

19:53.270 --> 19:59.320
that the three-part test that I mentioned is indeed coming to pass,

19:59.320 --> 20:02.740
then we could move later this year, we could begin later this year.

20:02.740 --> 20:07.360
But even if we do that, the subsequent steps will be dependent

20:07.360 --> 20:09.180
on continued progress in the economy.

20:09.180 --> 20:13.350
So we are tied to the data-we don't have a fixed calendar schedule-but we do

20:13.350 --> 20:18.570
have the same basic framework that I described in June.

20:18.570 --> 20:24.480
The criterion for ending the asset purchases program is a substantial

20:24.480 --> 20:26.810
improvement in the outlook for the labor market.

20:26.810 --> 20:33.080
Last time, I gave 7 percent as an indicative number to give you some sense of,

20:33.080 --> 20:34.790
you know, where that might be.

20:34.790 --> 20:37.200
But, as my first answer suggested,

20:37.200 --> 20:43.070
the unemployment rate is not necessarily a great measure, in all circumstances,

20:43.070 --> 20:45.550
of the state of the labor market overall.

20:45.550 --> 20:49.440
For example, just last month, the decline in the unemployment rate came

20:49.440 --> 20:53.770
about more than entirely because of declining participation,

20:53.770 --> 20:58.490
not because of increased jobs.

20:58.490 --> 21:03.060
So what we will be looking at is the overall labor market situation,

21:03.060 --> 21:06.850
including the unemployment rate, but including other factors as well.

21:06.850 --> 21:11.510
But, in particular, there is not any magic number that we are shooting for.

21:11.510 --> 21:18.680
We're looking for overall improvement in the labor market.

21:19.710 --> 21:20.080
STEVE LIESMAN.

21:20.080 --> 21:21.450
Steve Liesman, with CNBC.

21:21.450 --> 21:25.400
Mr. Chairman, one question-just three parts, if you don't mind.

21:25.400 --> 21:29.630
Have you indicated to President Obama you did not want to serve a third term?

21:29.630 --> 21:31.040
If so, when?

21:31.040 --> 21:32.440
Or did President Obama indicate

21:32.440 --> 21:35.190
to you he did not want you to serve a third term?

21:35.190 --> 21:37.070
And those two parts notwithstanding,

21:37.070 --> 21:39.760
would you serve a third term if asked, either wholly or in part?

21:39.760 --> 21:40.660
Thank you.

21:40.660 --> 21:41.510
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

21:41.510 --> 21:43.550
Well, it's convenient because I have the same answer

21:43.550 --> 21:46.920
to all three parts of your question.

21:46.920 --> 21:48.580
If you will indulge me just a little longer,

21:48.580 --> 21:51.020
I prefer not to talk about my plans at this point.

21:51.020 --> 21:56.060
I hope to have more information for you at some reasonably soon date,

21:56.060 --> 21:58.850
but today I want to focus on monetary policy.

21:58.850 --> 22:01.420
I'd prefer not to talk about my own plans.

22:06.550 --> 22:08.760
YLAN MUI. Ylan Mui, Washington Post.

22:08.760 --> 22:13.430
You mentioned that tighter fiscal conditions are a concern for the Committee

22:13.430 --> 22:14.750
as you guys think about whether

22:14.750 --> 22:17.650
or not it's appropriate to reduce asset purchases.

22:17.650 --> 22:22.150
What do you all expect to be able to do in the future,

22:22.150 --> 22:25.180
when you actually do begin to pull back in your asset purchases,

22:25.180 --> 22:27.740
to manage expectations and manage the market reactions

22:27.740 --> 22:30.800
such that we don't see another increase in rates?

22:30.800 --> 22:32.160
CHAIRMAN BERNAKE.

22:32.160 --> 22:35.900
What's the relationship between the pullback and fiscal policy?

22:35.900 --> 22:36.430
YLAN MUI. Oh, I'm sorry.

22:36.430 --> 22:40.730
I meant financial conditions, tighter financial conditions.

22:40.730 --> 22:41.250
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

22:41.250 --> 22:43.310
Oh, tighter financial conditions, sure.

22:43.310 --> 22:48.830
Well, I think part of the reaction we've seen-and it comes from a number

22:48.830 --> 22:55.020
of sources-part of it comes from improved economic news and that's part

22:55.020 --> 22:57.590
of the reason why rates have gone up in other countries

22:57.590 --> 22:58.750
as well as in the United States.

22:58.750 --> 23:03.200
And that-to the extent that tighter financial conditions reflect a better

23:03.200 --> 23:07.400
outlook, that's a good thing, and that's not a problem at all.

23:07.400 --> 23:10.660
Part of it reflects views about monetary policy,

23:10.660 --> 23:13.330
and that we want to make sure we get straight.

23:13.330 --> 23:17.120
And that's why-to answer the earlier question, again,

23:17.120 --> 23:18.650
it's why communication is so important.

23:18.650 --> 23:23.020
We need to explain, as best we can, how we're going to move

23:23.020 --> 23:25.840
and on what basis we're going to move.

23:25.840 --> 23:28.450
It's much more difficult today than it was 20 years ago

23:28.450 --> 23:31.480
because the tools are more complex so they're less familiar,

23:31.480 --> 23:33.040
but that's still very important.

23:33.040 --> 23:40.290
I think the other factor which was at play was an unwinding of excessively risky

23:40.290 --> 23:44.910
and levered positions in the markets, and insufficiencies of liquidity

23:44.910 --> 23:51.280
in some cases meant that those unwindings led to larger reactions in prices

23:51.280 --> 23:53.540
and rates than might otherwise have occurred.

23:53.540 --> 24:00.860
Now, the tightening associated with that is, to some extent, unwelcome, but,

24:00.860 --> 24:05.480
on the other hand, to the extent that some of the riskier,

24:05.480 --> 24:08.510
more levered positions have been eliminated,

24:08.510 --> 24:11.770
I think that makes the situation more sustainable and reduces, at least,

24:11.770 --> 24:15.830
the risk that there will be an over-strong reaction to further announcements.

24:15.830 --> 24:18.660
So we will do our best to communicate clearly.

24:18.660 --> 24:22.590
That is our goal and our objective.

24:22.590 --> 24:24.240
The more clearly we communicate,

24:24.240 --> 24:27.980
the better the chance that markets will understand our intentions

24:27.980 --> 24:31.250
and that we can avoid any sharp movements.

24:31.250 --> 24:37.170
But, again, we're dealing with tools that are less familiar, harder to quantify,

24:37.170 --> 24:41.250
and harder to communicate about than the traditional funds rate.

24:41.250 --> 24:43.100
ROBIN HARDING.

24:43.100 --> 24:49.930
Robin Harding, from the Financial Times.

24:49.930 --> 24:54.160
Mr. Chairman, the median Committee member expects a 2 percent interest rate

24:54.160 --> 24:57.890
at the end of 2016, and, in the long run,

24:57.890 --> 25:00.900
they expect interest rates to return to 4 percent.

25:00.900 --> 25:03.230
Can you give us any sense of when you

25:03.230 --> 25:05.590
and when the Committee expect interest rates

25:05.590 --> 25:07.640
to get back to that 4 percent figure?

25:07.640 --> 25:09.450
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

25:09.450 --> 25:13.180
Let me first restate the-I think the key point here,

25:13.180 --> 25:19.740
which is that the large majority of the participants of the FOMC,

25:19.740 --> 25:23.200
including voting and nonvoting members who are asked

25:23.200 --> 25:28.440
to describe their own assessment of optimal policy-the large majority

25:28.440 --> 25:32.920
of them estimate that the appropriate target for the federal funds rate

25:32.920 --> 25:35.610
at the end of 2016 will be around 2 percent,

25:35.610 --> 25:39.600
even though at that time the economy should be close to full employment,

25:39.600 --> 25:42.200
according to our best projections.

25:42.200 --> 25:45.730
The reason for that-there may be possibly several reasons,

25:45.730 --> 25:51.530
but we did discuss this in the Committee today-the primary reason

25:51.530 --> 25:56.640
for that low value is that we expect that a number

25:56.640 --> 26:01.010
of factors-including the slow recovery of the housing sector,

26:01.010 --> 26:05.410
continued fiscal drag, perhaps continued effects

26:05.410 --> 26:11.920
from the financial crisis-may still prove to be headwinds to the recovery.

26:11.920 --> 26:14.480
And even though we can achieve full employment,

26:14.480 --> 26:19.180
doing so will be done by using rates lower than, sort of, the long-run normal.

26:19.180 --> 26:23.380
So, in other words, in economics terms, the equilibrium rate,

26:23.380 --> 26:27.210
the rate that achieves full employment, looks like it will be lower for a time

26:27.210 --> 26:32.440
because of these headwinds that will be slowing aggregate demand growth.

26:32.440 --> 26:35.750
So that's why we expect to see growth that-I mean,

26:35.750 --> 26:39.330
rates at an unusually low level.

26:39.330 --> 26:41.350
I imagine it would take a few more years after that

26:41.350 --> 26:43.270
to get to the 4 percent level.

26:43.270 --> 26:45.780
I couldn't be much more precise than that-I mean,

26:45.780 --> 26:49.420
we're already obviously stretching the bounds of credibility to talk

26:49.420 --> 26:52.260
about specific projections to 2016.

26:52.260 --> 26:56.680
But I think you would expect to see that rates would gradually rise for the two

26:56.680 --> 27:03.370
or three years after 2016 and ultimately get to 4 percent.

27:03.370 --> 27:05.380
JOSH ZUMBRUN.

27:05.380 --> 27:07.570
Mr. Chairman, Josh Zumbrun from Bloomberg News.

27:07.570 --> 27:11.060
You indicated that you can see the Fed lowering the pace

27:11.060 --> 27:14.310
of purchases once the economy starts to grow faster,

27:14.310 --> 27:15.980
in line with what the Fed's projected.

27:15.980 --> 27:17.390
For about the past four years,

27:17.390 --> 27:19.870
the Fed has been projecting that growth would quicken

27:19.870 --> 27:22.780
to about 3 percent, and it never has.

27:22.780 --> 27:27.170
So, at what point are you decide-going to decide that other costs

27:27.170 --> 27:29.920
and benefits are the reason that you're making the decision?

27:29.920 --> 27:33.910
Are we getting close to that having to be a deciding factor even

27:33.910 --> 27:37.110
if you don't get the growth forecast, the way you haven't the past four years?

27:37.110 --> 27:39.840
And does the complication of this- this is kind of a second question,

27:39.840 --> 27:42.470
but I'm going to do it anyway-does the complication of this mean

27:42.470 --> 27:45.970
that you need a press conference to make a tapering decision?

27:45.970 --> 27:48.870
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

27:48.870 --> 27:53.370
Well, you're certainly right that we have been over-

27:53.370 --> 27:56.330
optimistic about out-year growth.

27:56.330 --> 28:00.360
There are a number of reasons for that.

28:00.360 --> 28:06.350
One reason for it, though, is that it appears-and I talked about this

28:06.350 --> 28:08.680
in a speech last year-it appears that,

28:08.680 --> 28:12.750
as part of the aftermath of the financial crisis, that, at least temporarily,

28:12.750 --> 28:16.770
the potential growth rate of the economy has been slowed,

28:16.770 --> 28:20.690
perhaps because new businesses are not being formed at the same rate,

28:20.690 --> 28:25.680
innovation may not be translated into new technologies at the same rate,

28:25.680 --> 28:27.990
investment is slower, et cetera.

28:27.990 --> 28:32.510
So it appears, again, that the potential rate of growth

28:32.510 --> 28:37.510
of the economy has been slowed somewhat, at least temporarily,

28:37.510 --> 28:39.570
by the recession and the financial crisis,

28:39.570 --> 28:42.230
and you can see that in the slower productivity figures.

28:42.230 --> 28:47.170
Now, we have-you know, we haven't anticipated that slowdown in productivity,

28:47.170 --> 28:53.250
and that's one of the main reasons why we haven't anticipated the relatively

28:53.250 --> 28:54.630
slow growth.

28:54.630 --> 28:57.370
Now, it's important to recognize though

28:57.370 --> 29:02.500
that what monetary policy affects is not the potential rate of growth,

29:02.500 --> 29:09.000
long-run rate of growth, but rather the cyclical part, the deviation of output

29:09.000 --> 29:11.350
and employment from its normal level.

29:11.350 --> 29:15.340
And in predicting the amount of slack in the economy, so to speak,

29:15.340 --> 29:16.350
we've done a little better.

29:16.350 --> 29:19.100
Our predictions of unemployment, for example,

29:19.100 --> 29:23.510
have been better than our predictions of growth.

29:23.510 --> 29:26.650
And, in particular, one thing that's been quite striking is

29:26.650 --> 29:32.030
that unemployment-we were too pessimistic on unemployment for this year.

29:32.030 --> 29:39.580
Unemployment has fallen faster than we anticipated.

29:39.580 --> 29:43.290
So, in that respect, we were too pessimistic rather than too optimistic.

29:43.290 --> 29:46.090
So, we'll continue to do the best we can.

29:46.090 --> 29:51.180
We're looking, again, to see confirmation of our broader scenario,

29:51.180 --> 29:54.210
which basically is that we'll continue to see progress in the labor market,

29:54.210 --> 29:56.730
that growth will be sufficient to support that progress,

29:56.730 --> 29:59.760
and that inflation will be moving back towards target,

29:59.760 --> 30:04.070
and that's what will determine our policy decisions.

30:04.070 --> 30:08.910
In terms of press conferences,

30:08.910 --> 30:13.310
I think it's important to say that there's an understanding in the Committee

30:13.310 --> 30:18.220
that we've had for a while, that there are eight

30:18.220 --> 30:21.680
"real" meetings every year-that every meeting is a meeting

30:21.680 --> 30:26.070
in which any policy decision can be taken.

30:26.070 --> 30:30.050
And, should anything occur at a meeting without a scheduled press conference

30:30.050 --> 30:34.230
that requires additional explanation, we certainly could arrange a public,

30:34.230 --> 30:36.420
on-the-record conference call or some other way

30:36.420 --> 30:38.830
of answering the media's questions.

30:38.830 --> 30:46.490
GREG IP. Greg Ip of the Economist.

30:46.490 --> 30:48.560
You said that you-the Committee would be unlikely

30:48.560 --> 30:53.340
to raise the federal funds rate if inflation remains below target,

30:53.340 --> 30:56.820
but your own projections have inflation-that's the midpoint

30:56.820 --> 31:00.590
of your inflation projections-below your 2 percent target through 2016.

31:00.590 --> 31:02.460
So is that inconsistent with the liftoff

31:02.460 --> 31:04.380
in the federal funds rate in that period?

31:04.380 --> 31:06.390
And, related, is there a case to be made

31:06.390 --> 31:08.680
that your thresholds should be supplemented with a lower bound

31:08.680 --> 31:11.570
for the inflation rate-i.e., you won't tighten if inflation is

31:11.570 --> 31:13.420
at or below some lower bound?

31:13.420 --> 31:15.090
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

31:15.090 --> 31:17.180
So on the latter part, you know, of course,

31:17.180 --> 31:22.720
you're seeing interest rate projections and inflation projections separately.

31:22.720 --> 31:24.740
You're not seeing them combined by individuals.

31:24.740 --> 31:27.240
Each individual is making their own projection.

31:27.240 --> 31:32.610
I think you're right, I mean, that we should be very reluctant to raise rates

31:32.610 --> 31:35.450
if inflation remains persistently below target,

31:35.450 --> 31:39.890
and that's one of the reasons that I think we can be very patient

31:39.890 --> 31:45.000
in raising the federal funds rate since we have not seen any inflation pressure.

31:45.000 --> 31:50.890
On having an inflation floor, that would be in addition to the guidance.

31:50.890 --> 31:57.040
We are discussing how we might clarify the guidance on the federal funds rate.

31:57.040 --> 31:59.550
That is certainly one possibility.

31:59.550 --> 32:04.540
I guess an interesting question there is whether we need additional guidance

32:04.540 --> 32:07.740
on that, given that we do have a target.

32:07.740 --> 32:13.590
And, of course, implicit in our policy strategy is trying to reach that target

32:13.590 --> 32:18.390
for inflation, but that-an inflation floor is certainly something that,

32:18.390 --> 32:25.140
you know, could be a sensible modification or addition to the guidance.

32:25.140 --> 32:30.030
VICTORIA McGRANE.

32:30.030 --> 32:32.260
Victoria McGrane, Dow Jones Newswires.

32:32.260 --> 32:36.240
Many investors were expecting the Fed to move at least a little bit

32:36.240 --> 32:39.710
in pulling back the bond-buying program today.

32:39.710 --> 32:44.660
Given that you all decided not to do that, are-do you have any concerns that,

32:44.660 --> 32:50.340
once again, the Fed is confusing investors and sending mixed signals?

32:50.340 --> 32:53.030
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

32:53.030 --> 32:54.590
Well, I don't recall stating

32:54.590 --> 32:57.350
that we would do any particular thing in this meeting.

32:57.350 --> 33:00.300
What we are going to do is the right thing for the economy.

33:00.300 --> 33:08.010
And our assessment of the data since June is that, taken collectively,

33:08.010 --> 33:14.580
that it didn't quite meet the standard of satisfying our-or of ratifying

33:14.580 --> 33:20.400
or confirming our basic outlook for, again, increasing growth,

33:20.400 --> 33:26.450
improving labor markets, and inflation moving back towards target.

33:26.450 --> 33:29.280
We try our best to communicate to markets-we'll continue

33:29.280 --> 33:33.820
to do that-but we can't let market expectations dictate our policy actions.

33:33.820 --> 33:37.760
Our policy actions have to be determined by our best assessment

33:37.760 --> 33:41.610
of what's needed for the economy.

33:41.610 --> 33:43.240
PETER BARNES.

33:44.650 --> 33:46.190
Peter Barnes, Fox Business, sir.

33:46.190 --> 33:49.380
You mentioned fiscal issues in the statement today.

33:49.380 --> 33:52.230
Are you concerned about a government shutdown?

33:52.230 --> 33:54.560
We're hearing more about that possibility.

33:54.560 --> 33:58.620
Did that come up in your discussions at this meeting?

33:58.620 --> 34:03.320
What do you think would be the impact of a government shutdown on the economy,

34:03.320 --> 34:08.700
and what could the Fed's-or, would the Fed be prepared to respond to that

34:08.700 --> 34:12.290
and help the economy with additional accommodation, for example,

34:12.290 --> 34:15.140
additional asset purchases?

34:15.140 --> 34:15.570
Thank you.

34:15.570 --> 34:15.930
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

34:15.930 --> 34:17.700
Well, a factor that did concern us

34:17.700 --> 34:21.910
in our discussion was some upcoming fiscal policy decisions.

34:21.910 --> 34:24.790
I would include both the possibility of a government shutdown,

34:24.790 --> 34:26.780
but also the debt limit issue.

34:26.780 --> 34:32.220
These are obviously, you know,

34:32.220 --> 34:38.220
part of a very complicated set of legislative decisions, strategies, battles,

34:38.220 --> 34:40.550
et cetera, which I won't get into.

34:40.550 --> 34:46.290
But it is the case, I think, that a government shutdown-and perhaps,

34:46.290 --> 34:51.530
even more so, a failure to raise the debt limit-could have very serious

34:51.530 --> 34:55.580
consequences for the financial markets and for the economy,

34:55.580 --> 34:59.230
and the Federal Reserve's policy is to do whatever we can

34:59.230 --> 35:01.530
to keep the economy on course.

35:01.530 --> 35:05.270
And so, if these actions led the economy to slow,

35:05.270 --> 35:07.410
then we would have to take that into account, surely.

35:07.410 --> 35:12.060
So this is one of the risks that we are looking at as we think about policy.

35:12.060 --> 35:15.920
That being said, you know, again,

35:15.920 --> 35:19.910
our ability to offset these shocks is very limited,

35:19.910 --> 35:22.030
particularly a debt limit shock.

35:22.030 --> 35:25.440
And I think it's extraordinarily important that Congress

35:25.440 --> 35:27.910
and the Administration work together to find a way to make sure

35:27.910 --> 35:31.010
that the government is funded, public services are provided,

35:31.010 --> 35:35.780
that the government pays its bills, and that we avoid any kind of event

35:35.780 --> 35:38.500
like 2011, which had, at least for a time,

35:38.500 --> 35:44.210
a noticeable adverse effect on confidence and on the economy.

35:44.210 --> 35:46.660
ANNALYN KURTZ.

35:46.660 --> 35:49.400
Annalyn Kurtz, with CNNMoney.

35:49.400 --> 35:54.220
This week marks five years since the financial crisis began, and Hank Paulson,

35:54.220 --> 35:57.260
who you've worked very closely with, has said his biggest regret was

35:57.260 --> 36:00.910
that he wasn't able to convince the American people that what was done-

36:00.910 --> 36:04.620
the bank bailouts-weren't for Wall Street, they were for Main Street.

36:04.620 --> 36:08.220
What is your biggest regret as you reflect on the five-year anniversary?

36:08.220 --> 36:10.450
And do you believe that the Fed, Congress,

36:10.450 --> 36:14.190
and the President have put the necessary measures in place

36:14.190 --> 36:17.020
to prevent another deep financial crisis?

36:17.020 --> 36:19.110
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

36:19.110 --> 36:23.550
Well, on regrets, as Frank Sinatra says, I have many.

36:23.550 --> 36:27.860
I think my-you know, reasonably,

36:27.860 --> 36:31.640
the biggest regret I have is that we didn't forestall the crisis.

36:31.640 --> 36:37.740
I think once the crisis got going, it was extremely hard to prevent.

36:37.740 --> 36:42.650
You know, I think we did what we could, given the powers that we had,

36:42.650 --> 36:48.180
and I would agree with Hank that we were motivated entirely by the interest

36:48.180 --> 36:53.730
of the broader public, that our goal was to stabilize the financial system

36:53.730 --> 36:55.480
so that it would not bring the economy down,

36:55.480 --> 37:03.410
so that it would not create massive unemployment and economic hardship

37:03.410 --> 37:06.470
that was even more-that would have been even more severe

37:06.470 --> 37:11.340
by many times than what we actually saw.

37:11.340 --> 37:14.090
So I agree with him on that.

37:14.090 --> 37:18.880
And I guess, since you gave me the opportunity, I would mention that, of course,

37:18.880 --> 37:22.790
all the money that was used in those operations has been paid back

37:22.790 --> 37:23.340
with interest.

37:23.340 --> 37:28.690
And so, it hasn't been costly, even from a fiscal point of view.

37:28.690 --> 37:33.150
Now, in terms of progress, that's a good question.

37:33.150 --> 37:36.060
I think we've made a lot of progress.

37:36.060 --> 37:40.370
We had, of course, the Dodd-Frank law passed in 2010, and then we recently,

37:40.370 --> 37:44.880
you know, have come to agreement internationally on a number of measures,

37:44.880 --> 37:51.010
including Basel III and other agreements relating to the shadow banking system

37:51.010 --> 37:53.500
and other aspects of the financial system.

37:53.500 --> 37:59.860
I think that our-today, our large financial firms, for example,

37:59.860 --> 38:03.800
are better capitalized by far than they were certainly during the crisis

38:03.800 --> 38:06.500
and even before the crisis.

38:06.500 --> 38:07.750
Supervision is tougher.

38:07.750 --> 38:10.300
We do stress testing to make sure

38:10.300 --> 38:14.950
that firms can withstand not only normal shocks, but very, very large shocks,

38:14.950 --> 38:18.450
similar to those they experienced in 2008.

38:18.450 --> 38:21.770
And, very importantly, of course, we now have a tool that we didn't have

38:21.770 --> 38:23.630
in 2008-which would have made, I think,

38:23.630 --> 38:27.270
a significant difference if we had had it-which is the orderly liquidation

38:27.270 --> 38:29.910
authority that the Dodd-Frank bill gave to the FDIC

38:29.910 --> 38:32.020
in collaboration with the Fed.

38:32.020 --> 38:39.000
Under the orderly liquidation authority, the FDIC, with other agencies,

38:39.000 --> 38:45.390
has the ability to wind down a failing financial firm in a way

38:45.390 --> 38:50.040
that minimizes the direct impact on the financial markets and on the economy.

38:50.040 --> 38:51.090
Now, I should say, I don't want

38:51.090 --> 38:53.910
to overstate the case-I think there's a lot more work to be done.

38:53.910 --> 38:57.520
In the case of resolution regimes, for example,

38:57.520 --> 39:00.750
the United States has set the course internationally.

39:00.750 --> 39:05.080
Other countries and international bodies like the FSB are setting up standards

39:05.080 --> 39:08.620
for resolution regimes, which are very similar to those of the United States,

39:08.620 --> 39:11.890
which is going to make for better cooperation across borders.

39:11.890 --> 39:16.800
But we're still some distance from being fully geared up to work

39:16.800 --> 39:21.870
with foreign counterparts to successfully wind

39:21.870 --> 39:26.030
down an international-multinational financial firm.

39:26.030 --> 39:27.970
And that's-we've made progress in that direction,

39:27.970 --> 39:29.260
but we need to do more, I think.

39:29.260 --> 39:32.060
So, I think there's more to be done.

39:32.060 --> 39:33.450
There's more to be done on derivatives,

39:33.450 --> 39:36.220
although a lot has been done to make them more transparent

39:36.220 --> 39:38.830
and to make the trading of derivatives safer.

39:38.830 --> 39:43.200
But it's going to be probably some time before, you know,

39:43.200 --> 39:46.130
all of this stuff that has been undertaken,

39:46.130 --> 39:49.320
all of these measures are fully implemented.

39:49.320 --> 39:54.200
And we can assess, you know, the ultimate impact on the financial system.

39:54.200 --> 40:00.290
STEVE BECKNER.

40:00.290 --> 40:02.610
Steve Beckner of MNI, Mr. Chairman.

40:02.610 --> 40:07.080
A number of economists, and, indeed, some of your Fed colleagues,

40:07.080 --> 40:09.640
have argued that the effectiveness

40:09.640 --> 40:14.900
of quantitative easing has greatly diminished, if not disappeared,

40:14.900 --> 40:21.370
and they point to the recent performance of the economy as proof of that.

40:21.370 --> 40:24.580
And there have been a number of people who have argued that there are regulatory

40:24.580 --> 40:29.900
and other impediments to growth beyond the reach of monetary policy.

40:29.900 --> 40:31.850
To what extent are these valid arguments?

40:31.850 --> 40:36.960
And if the economy does not speed up, if it does not reach your objectives,

40:36.960 --> 40:39.540
how will you ever get out of quantitative easing?

40:39.540 --> 40:41.850
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

40:41.850 --> 40:46.620
Well, on the effectiveness of our asset purchases,

40:46.620 --> 40:50.020
it's difficult to get a precise measure.

40:50.020 --> 40:52.730
There's a large academic literature on this subject,

40:52.730 --> 40:54.730
and they have a range of results,

40:54.730 --> 40:56.690
some suggesting that this is a quite powerful tool,

40:56.690 --> 40:58.480
some that it's less powerful.

40:58.480 --> 41:00.670
My own assessment is that it has been effective.

41:00.670 --> 41:07.530
If you look at the recovery, you see that some of the strongest sectors,

41:07.530 --> 41:09.350
the leading sectors like housing and autos,

41:09.350 --> 41:10.860
have been interest- sensitive sectors.

41:10.860 --> 41:13.380
And that these policies have been successful

41:13.380 --> 41:16.500
in strengthening financial conditions, lowering interest rates,

41:16.500 --> 41:19.340
and thereby promoting recovery.

41:19.340 --> 41:22.580
So I do think that they have been effective.

41:22.580 --> 41:24.550
You mentioned that there hasn't been any progress.

41:24.550 --> 41:26.440
There has been a lot of progress.

41:26.440 --> 41:30.030
As I said at the beginning, labor market indicators,

41:30.030 --> 41:34.160
while still not where we'd like them to be, are much better today than they were

41:34.160 --> 41:37.970
when we began this latest program a year ago.

41:37.970 --> 41:39.870
And, importantly-as actually is referenced

41:39.870 --> 41:45.130
in our FOMC statement-that happened notwithstanding a set of fiscal policies

41:45.130 --> 41:50.340
which the CBO said would cost between 1 and 1½ percentage points of real growth

41:50.340 --> 41:52.510
and hundreds of thousands of jobs.

41:52.510 --> 42:00.220
So the fact that we have maintained improvements in the labor market that are

42:00.220 --> 42:04.970
as good or better than the previous year, notwithstanding this fiscal drag,

42:04.970 --> 42:09.950
is some indication that there is at least a partial offset from monetary policy.

42:09.950 --> 42:13.940
Now, just as you say, there are a lot of things in the economy

42:13.940 --> 42:17.590
that monetary policy can't address.

42:17.590 --> 42:23.510
They include the effectiveness of regulation, they include fiscal policy,

42:23.510 --> 42:28.250
they include developments in the private sector.

42:28.250 --> 42:31.060
We do what we can do and what-if we can get help,

42:31.060 --> 42:34.060
we're delighted to have help from other policymakers and from the private sector

42:34.060 --> 42:37.180
and we hope that that will happen.

42:37.180 --> 42:42.590
The criterion for ending asset purchases is not, you know,

42:42.590 --> 42:43.940
some very high rate of growth.

42:43.940 --> 42:46.280
What it is is the criterion-let me just remind you,

42:46.280 --> 42:50.360
the criterion is a substantial improvement in the outlook for the labor market,

42:50.360 --> 42:52.840
and we have made significant improvement.

42:52.840 --> 42:56.580
Ultimately, we will reach that level of substantial improvement,

42:56.580 --> 43:00.900
and at that point, we will be able to wind down the asset purchases.

43:00.900 --> 43:05.670
Again, you know, and I think people don't fully appreciate

43:05.670 --> 43:08.860
that we have two tools: We have asset purchases,

43:08.860 --> 43:12.180
and we have rate policy and guidance about rates.

43:12.180 --> 43:14.410
It's our view that the latter, the rate policy,

43:14.410 --> 43:17.070
is actually the stronger, more reliable tool.

43:17.070 --> 43:20.480
And when we get to the point where we can, you know,

43:20.480 --> 43:22.750
where we are close enough to full employment

43:22.750 --> 43:26.390
that rate policy will be sufficient, I think that we will still be able

43:26.390 --> 43:29.530
to provide-even if asset purchases are reduced-we will still be able

43:29.530 --> 43:33.010
to provide a highly accommodative monetary background

43:33.010 --> 43:35.880
that will allow the economy to continue to grow

43:35.880 --> 43:38.270
and move towards full employment.

43:38.270 --> 43:43.730
DONNA BORAK.

43:43.730 --> 43:45.910
Chairman, Donna Borak, with American Banker.

43:45.910 --> 43:49.690
The Financial Stability Oversight Council has already designated two nonbank

43:49.690 --> 43:53.020
firms as SIFIs, as you know, and potentially a third very soon,

43:53.020 --> 43:55.270
and presumably others to follow.

43:55.270 --> 43:56.960
However, little has been said in terms

43:56.960 --> 44:00.160
of how specifically these firms will be regulated by the Fed,

44:00.160 --> 44:03.490
which has been a chief criticism of the entire process.

44:03.490 --> 44:06.610
Given that, can you provide us any guidance at this point in terms

44:06.610 --> 44:08.380
of how far along the Fed is in terms

44:08.380 --> 44:12.290
of letting the banks know how they will be regulated besides,

44:12.290 --> 44:13.560
you know, tailoring the plans?

44:13.560 --> 44:14.770
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

44:14.770 --> 44:19.430
Well, the two firms that have been designated, AIG and GE Capital,

44:19.430 --> 44:24.460
actually are-have been regulated by the Fed, because both of them are savings

44:24.460 --> 44:27.020
and loan, thrift holding companies.

44:27.020 --> 44:29.250
So we have a lot of already experience with those firms

44:29.250 --> 44:32.550
and a lot of contact with those firms.

44:32.550 --> 44:37.820
We will-I want to use the word "tailored" because we want to design a regime

44:37.820 --> 44:42.480
that is appropriate for the business model of the particular firm.

44:42.480 --> 44:46.060
But our other objective, and what makes designation

44:46.060 --> 44:49.350
by the FSOC particularly noteworthy,

44:49.350 --> 44:54.130
is that the primary goal of the consolidated supervision by the Fed is

44:54.130 --> 45:01.140
to make sure that the firms-the firm doesn't in any way endanger the stability

45:01.140 --> 45:02.970
of the broad financial system.

45:02.970 --> 45:09.180
So we'll be looking at not just the usual safety and soundness type matters

45:09.180 --> 45:14.680
or supervision, which both can be, again, tailored to the types of assets

45:14.680 --> 45:19.730
and liabilities that the firm has, but also we're going to want to focus

45:19.730 --> 45:27.310
on things like resolution authority, practices relating to derivatives

45:27.310 --> 45:30.250
and other exposures, interconnectedness, et cetera,

45:30.250 --> 45:34.880
to make sure that the firm in its structure

45:34.880 --> 45:37.600
and in its operations doesn't pose a threat to the wider system.

45:37.600 --> 45:42.050
And that's what is going to be distinctive about our oversight,

45:42.050 --> 45:46.760
not only of these designated firms, but also of the large bank holding companies

45:46.760 --> 45:50.150
that we already oversee and which we are already subjecting

45:50.150 --> 45:58.120
to tougher supervision, higher capital, stress tests, and all the rest.

46:00.570 --> 46:01.570
PETER COOK.

46:01.570 --> 46:03.380
Peter Cook of Bloomberg Television, Mr. Chairman.

46:03.380 --> 46:06.040
One of my colleagues was remarking as we came in here,

46:06.040 --> 46:08.990
we don't often get surprises from the Federal Reserve.

46:08.990 --> 46:13.030
This was a surprise, you talked about-you hadn't telegraphed anything

46:13.030 --> 46:15.740
specifically, but you've seen the market reaction, I'm sure.

46:15.740 --> 46:19.040
My question for you is, were you intending a surprise today,

46:19.040 --> 46:22.680
and did you get the intended result, judging from the market reaction?

46:22.680 --> 46:26.500
And related to that, by taking this action today-continuing the bond purchases

46:26.500 --> 46:29.470
going forward-at what point do you believe you're starting

46:29.470 --> 46:31.300
to complicate the exit strategy?

46:31.300 --> 46:35.170
Simply by continuing to keep the Fed's foot on the gas pedal,

46:35.170 --> 46:38.400
do you make life more complicated for the Federal Reserve down the road?

46:38.400 --> 46:40.750
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:40.750 --> 46:44.360
Well, it's our intention to try to set policy as appropriate

46:44.360 --> 46:48.220
for the economy, as I said earlier.

46:48.220 --> 46:51.300
We are somewhat concerned.

46:51.300 --> 46:56.090
I won't overstate it, but we do want to see the effects of higher interest rates

46:56.090 --> 46:59.290
on the economy, particularly in mortgage rates, on housing.

46:59.290 --> 47:05.590
So to the extent that our policy makes conditions-our policy decision today

47:05.590 --> 47:10.540
makes conditions just a little bit easier, that's desirable.

47:10.540 --> 47:15.840
We want to make sure that the economy has adequate support and, in particular,

47:15.840 --> 47:21.080
it's less surprising the market or easing policy as it is avoiding a tightening

47:21.080 --> 47:26.130
until we can be comfortable that the economy is in fact growing, you know,

47:26.130 --> 47:27.430
the way we want it to be growing.

47:27.430 --> 47:34.720
So this was a step-it was a step, a precautionary step if you will.

47:34.720 --> 47:42.030
It was a-the intention is to wait a bit longer

47:42.030 --> 47:46.170
and to try to get confirming evidence whether to the-to whether

47:46.170 --> 47:51.380
or not the economy is, in fact, conforming to this general outlook that we have.

47:51.380 --> 48:00.300
I don't think that we are complicating anything for future FOMCs.

48:00.300 --> 48:05.440
It's true that the assets that we've been buying add

48:05.440 --> 48:07.340
to the size of our balance sheet.

48:07.340 --> 48:12.570
But we have developed a variety of tools, and we think we have numerous tools

48:12.570 --> 48:16.180
that we-can be used to both manage interest rates

48:16.180 --> 48:22.860
and to ultimately unwind the balance sheet when the time comes.

48:22.860 --> 48:26.880
So I, you know, I'm-I feel quite comfortable that we can-in particular,

48:26.880 --> 48:29.490
that we can raise interest rates at the appropriate time,

48:29.490 --> 48:33.600
even if the balance sheet remains large for an extended period.

48:33.600 --> 48:38.300
And that will be true, of course, for, you know, future FOMCs as well.

48:39.810 --> 48:42.550
KATE DAVIDSON.

48:42.550 --> 48:45.250
Mr. Chairman, Kate Davidson from Politico.

48:45.250 --> 48:47.720
Do you think that all of the recent attention being paid

48:47.720 --> 48:51.590
to who will be your replacement has had any immediate effect on the Fed,

48:51.590 --> 48:55.230
and could it have any lingering effect on your successor?

48:55.230 --> 48:57.950
And also, do you think the process has just become too politicized,

48:57.950 --> 49:00.360
or is this part of a healthy debate?

49:00.360 --> 49:01.590
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

49:01.590 --> 49:05.490
I think the Federal Reserve has strong institutional credibility.

49:05.490 --> 49:10.250
And it is a strong institution, a highly competent institution,

49:10.250 --> 49:16.240
and it's independent, it's nonpartisan, and I'm not particularly concerned

49:16.240 --> 49:20.280
about the political environment for the Federal Reserve.

49:20.280 --> 49:25.210
I think the Fed will be-continue to be an important institution

49:25.210 --> 49:31.350
in the United States, and that it will maintain its independence going forward.

49:31.350 --> 49:33.460
GREG ROBB.

49:36.030 --> 49:40.130
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

49:40.130 --> 49:41.450
Greg Robb, MarketWatch.

49:41.450 --> 49:44.510
Was there a discussion among the Committee today

49:44.510 --> 49:48.430
about changing the forward guidance, the 6.5 percent jobless rate?

49:48.430 --> 49:51.880
And could you say why the Committee decided to hold that steady

49:51.880 --> 49:54.830
in light of the weaker economy?

49:54.830 --> 49:56.230
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

49:56.230 --> 49:57.510
Well, as I mentioned earlier,

49:57.510 --> 50:03.190
the Committee has regularly reviewed the forward guidance,

50:03.190 --> 50:05.230
and there are a number of ways

50:05.230 --> 50:07.730
in which the forward guidance could be strengthened.

50:07.730 --> 50:12.400
For example, Mr. Ip mentioned an inflation floor.

50:12.400 --> 50:14.700
There are other steps that we could take.

50:14.700 --> 50:18.210
We could provide more information about what happens after we get to 6.5 percent

50:18.210 --> 50:20.420
and those sorts of things, and to the extent

50:20.420 --> 50:24.970
that we could provide precise guidance, I think that would be desirable.

50:24.970 --> 50:29.500
Now, it's very important that we not take any of these steps lightly,

50:29.500 --> 50:32.590
that we make sure we understand all the implications,

50:32.590 --> 50:37.370
and that we are comfortable that it will be-that any modifications

50:37.370 --> 50:41.440
of the guidance will be credible to markets and to the public.

50:41.440 --> 50:43.840
So, we continue to think about options.

50:43.840 --> 50:47.170
There are a number of options that we have talked about.

50:47.170 --> 50:55.140
But today, we-as of today, we didn't choose to make any changes to the guidance.

50:55.140 --> 51:01.980
DON LEE. Don Lee, L.A. Times.

51:01.980 --> 51:07.400
As you may know, the Census Bureau reported yesterday that the poverty rate

51:07.400 --> 51:11.290
and the median household income saw no improvement last year.

51:11.290 --> 51:18.470
And I wonder, when you see median incomes turning up significantly

51:18.470 --> 51:22.920
for most people, and in light of the fact that people in the middle

51:22.920 --> 51:26.140
and the bottom have seen very little of the gains relative

51:26.140 --> 51:28.960
to higher income households,

51:28.960 --> 51:33.490
how would you assess the-both quantitative easing and Fed policies?

51:33.490 --> 51:34.450
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

51:34.450 --> 51:40.950
Sure. So that's certainly the case that there are too many people in poverty.

51:40.950 --> 51:44.140
There are a lot of complex issues involved.

51:44.140 --> 51:48.830
There are complex measurement issues, I would just have to mention that.

51:48.830 --> 51:53.890
There are a lot of issues that are really long-term issues as well.

51:53.890 --> 52:00.910
For example, it might seem a puzzle that the U.S. economy gets richer

52:00.910 --> 52:03.800
and richer, and yet there are more poor people.

52:03.800 --> 52:08.670
And the explanation, of course, is that our economy is becoming more unequal.

52:08.670 --> 52:13.460
The more very rich people and more people in the lower half

52:13.460 --> 52:17.910
who are not doing well, these are- there's a lot of reasons behind this trend,

52:17.910 --> 52:20.650
which has been going on for decades,

52:20.650 --> 52:24.860
and economists disagree about the relative importance of things like technology

52:24.860 --> 52:27.980
and international trade and unionization and other factors

52:27.980 --> 52:29.480
that have contributed to that.

52:29.480 --> 52:33.550
But I guess my first point is that these long-run trends-it's important

52:33.550 --> 52:37.270
to address these trends, but the Federal Reserve doesn't really have the tools

52:37.270 --> 52:39.460
to address these long-run distributional trends.

52:39.460 --> 52:43.410
They can only be addressed really by Congress and by the Administration.

52:43.410 --> 52:48.270
And it's up to them, I think, to take those steps.

52:48.270 --> 52:54.040
The Federal Reserve is-we are doing our part to help the median family,

52:54.040 --> 52:56.870
the median American, because one

52:56.870 --> 52:59.170
of our principal goals-we have two principal goals,

52:59.170 --> 53:02.140
one is maximum employment, jobs.

53:02.140 --> 53:07.220
The best way to help families is to create employment opportunities.

53:07.220 --> 53:09.360
We're still not satisfied, obviously,

53:09.360 --> 53:11.880
with where the labor market, the job market is.

53:11.880 --> 53:14.980
We'll continue to try to provide support for that.

53:14.980 --> 53:19.110
And then the other goal is price stability, low inflation, which, of course,

53:19.110 --> 53:25.270
also helps make the economy work better for people in the middle

53:25.270 --> 53:28.160
and the lower parts of the distribution.

53:28.160 --> 53:30.480
So we use the tools that we have.

53:30.480 --> 53:35.650
It would be better to have a mix of tools at work-not just monetary policy,

53:35.650 --> 53:38.590
but fiscal policy and other policies as well.

53:38.590 --> 53:41.740
But the Federal Reserve, we can-you know, we only have a certain set of tools,

53:41.740 --> 53:43.510
and those are the ones that we use.

53:43.510 --> 53:47.430
Again, our objective-our objectives of creating jobs

53:47.430 --> 53:48.950
and maintaining price stability, I think,

53:48.950 --> 53:52.060
are quite consistent with helping the average American,

53:52.060 --> 53:55.590
but there's limits to what we can do about long- run trends,

53:55.590 --> 53:59.640
and I think those are very important issues that Congress

53:59.640 --> 54:03.910
and the Administration, you know, need to look at and decide, you know,

54:03.910 --> 54:10.760
what needs to be done there.

54:10.760 --> 54:12.550
JEREMY TORDJMAN.

54:12.550 --> 54:15.690
Hi, Jeremy Tordjman, with AFP.

54:15.690 --> 54:20.440
Some emerging countries are blaming the Fed policies for the financial distress

54:20.440 --> 54:22.100
that they are experiencing.

54:22.100 --> 54:23.750
I wanted to have your take on that.

54:23.750 --> 54:29.380
And also, how did you judge the way the markets reacted

54:29.380 --> 54:32.530
to your tapering announcement back in June?

54:32.530 --> 54:33.090
Thank you very much.

54:33.090 --> 54:34.390
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

54:34.390 --> 54:35.960
Well, let me just talk about-I talked a little bit

54:35.960 --> 54:38.080
about the communication in June.

54:38.080 --> 54:39.760
Let me talk just about the emerging markets,

54:39.760 --> 54:41.580
which I think is an important issue.

54:41.580 --> 54:47.270
Let me just first say that we have a lot of economists who spend all

54:47.270 --> 54:51.170
of their time looking at international aspects of monetary policy,

54:51.170 --> 54:54.550
and we spend a lot of time looking at emerging markets.

54:54.550 --> 54:57.540
I spend a lot of time talking to my colleagues in emerging markets.

54:57.540 --> 54:58.830
So we're watching that very carefully.

54:58.830 --> 55:03.090
The United States is part of a globally integrated economic

55:03.090 --> 55:08.090
and financial system, and problems in emerging markets-or in any country,

55:08.090 --> 55:12.560
for that matter-can affect the United States as well.

55:12.560 --> 55:16.130
And so, again, we are watching those developments very carefully.

55:16.130 --> 55:19.980
It is true that changes in longer-term interest rates

55:19.980 --> 55:23.830
in the United States-but also in other advanced economies-does have some effect

55:23.830 --> 55:26.710
on emerging markets, particularly those who are trying

55:26.710 --> 55:31.950
to peg their exchange rate, and can lead to some capital inflows or outflows.

55:31.950 --> 55:37.530
But there are also other factors that affect inflows and outflows.

55:37.530 --> 55:42.550
Those include changes in risk preference by investors,

55:42.550 --> 55:47.440
changes in growth expectations, different perceptions of institutional strength

55:47.440 --> 55:52.140
within emerging markets across different countries.

55:52.140 --> 55:55.650
So there are a lot of factors that are there playing a role,

55:55.650 --> 55:58.950
and that's one reason why different emerging markets have had

55:58.950 --> 55:59.930
different experiences.

55:59.930 --> 56:04.250
They have different institutional structures and different policies.

56:04.250 --> 56:07.740
But, just to come to the bottom line here,

56:07.740 --> 56:15.440
we think it's very important that emerging markets grow and are prosperous.

56:15.440 --> 56:17.460
We pay close attention to what's happening

56:17.460 --> 56:23.610
in those countries-it affects the United States.

56:23.610 --> 56:25.850
The main point, I guess, I would end with, though,

56:25.850 --> 56:30.790
is that what we're trying to do with our monetary policy here is, I think,

56:30.790 --> 56:33.140
my colleagues in the emerging markets recognize,

56:33.140 --> 56:36.620
is trying to create a stronger U.S. economy.

56:36.620 --> 56:39.600
And a stronger U.S. economy is one of the most important things

56:39.600 --> 56:44.700
that could happen to help the economies of emerging markets.

56:44.700 --> 56:47.740
And, again, I think my colleagues in many

56:47.740 --> 56:52.880
of the emerging markets appreciate that-notwithstanding some of the effects

56:52.880 --> 56:56.990
that they may have felt-that efforts to strengthen the U.S. economy

56:56.990 --> 57:01.650
and other advanced economies in Europe and elsewhere ultimately redounds

57:01.650 --> 57:05.890
to the benefit of the global economy, including emerging markets as well.

57:05.890 --> 57:07.330
Thank you.

